Put a web server online and everyone starts trying to hack it. Why? A basic rule of economics. Short supply, big demand - there aren’t enough web servers for everyone. Let’s run a web server for every human on the planet, all 8 billion of them. I couldn’t get funding, so we’re bootstrapping. The goal is to run 15 million web servers on one VM with 10GB of RAM and 1 CPU core. If we succeed, I’ll find some RAM from old microwaves around the house and we’ll scale it up so everyone can have their own web server. Demand will drop to zero and cybersecurity will be solved once and for all. Web server

Instead of vibe coding it all, let’s use an ancient technique called learning . First of all, what is a web server? There are a few essential syscalls. listen - syscall, asks kernel to listen for connections on a socket accept - syscall, accepts a connection on a socket recv - receives data from a socket send - sends data close - closes What is a syscall? A function that is executed by the OS kernel. Whatever fancy language or library you use for HTTP, it all comes down to those syscalls (well, yes, but actually no). Let’s use C to make it all explicit. We start listening, then read from the socket in a loop and always reply with OK . int main ( void )

// We use port 0 which makes OS return any available port

int server = socket ( AF_INET , SOCK_STREAM , 0 );

const char reply [] = "HTTP/1.0 200 OK \r\n Content-Length: 2 \r\n\r\n OK" ;

for ( int client ; ( client = accept ( server , NULL , NULL )) >= 0 ;) {

if ( recv ( client , NULL , 4096 , MSG_TRUNC ) > 0 )

send ( client , reply , sizeof reply - 1 , MSG_NOSIGNAL );

} It couldn’t be simpler: listen on a socket, accept a connection, read the data, send a reply, close the client connection, and repeat. It gives us around 65k RPS. Now that we’ve learned everything, let’s finish the article and call it a day: $ for i in $( seq 1 15_000_000 ); do echo $i ; ./server &; done

program exited with error 1 Hm, why did listen fail? Ah, we’ve run out of ports! We’re using port=0 , so the kernel picks an available port from ip_local_port_range . $ sysctl net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range

net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range = 32768 60999 Why are so many ports skipped? Historically, port 0 was created for outbound connections. When you call connect('example.com', 80) , Linux connects to the remote server (the sending part) and then allocates a random port on the host from ip_local_port_range to receive data (the receiving part). There are a few reasons the default range is small. Port 0 is special. You cannot listen on it (actually, you can - https://acid.vegas/blog/the-port-that-does-not-exist/ ). Ports 1-1024 require root. Ports 1024-32768 are skipped to keep dynamically allocated ports from conflicting with existing apps. You don’t want to call connect('example.com') and end up allocating port 3306 to receive incoming data. mysql cannot start because you have example.com open in your Firefox would make for a confusing UX. Ports 60999 - 65535 are excluded for historical reasons dating back to IP Masquerading. Nothing prevents us from overriding the range and using 1-65535 , but that’s still a bit short of 15 million. A UDP server and a TCP server can use the same port, so we can double that to 128k. We could run a proxy in front to forward requests to the right process (virtual hosting, yay), but these are all ancient techniques - in the AI era, whenever you have a software problem, you just throw money at the hardware. Let’s simply connect 15M/32k=450 new network cards to the server. The 65k limit comes from the TCP standard. A port is a u16 , so even if we hack the kernel with find '*.c' -exec sed -i 's/u16/u32/' \; to support bigger port numbers, normal clients still wouldn’t be able to connect. Each listening server is identified by a (protocol, IP, port) tuple. That explains how we can use the same port for UDP and TCP. It also means we can use a bunch of different IPs for our web servers. If we…
